      "nuzest protein powder",
      "nuzest clean lean protein review",
      "is nuzest third party tested",
      "nuzest heavy metals"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Pea Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-pea-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Label Decoded"} badge="Research-Backed" author={author} dates={dates} label="Our standards">
      <Fragment slot="title">Is Nuzest Protein Powder Good?</Fragment>
      Nuzest sells one of the shortest labels in protein powder and talks a lot about heavy metal testing. Here is what the label and the testing claims actually tell you, and what a scoop really costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#testing">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#heavy-metals">Heavy Metals</a>
      <a href="#protein-quality">Protein Quality</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#who-for">Who It Suits</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Nuzest Clean Lean Protein is a good plant protein with one of the shortest labels you can buy: in the Smooth Vanilla flavor, pea protein isolate, natural flavors and thaumatin, a sweet protein from the katemfe fruit, and nothing else. Nuzest says every product is tested by ISO 17025 accredited labs for heavy metals, pesticides and glyphosate, and it is unusually open about heavy metals. The drawbacks are a modest 20 g of protein per serving, no sport certification such as NSF or Informed Sport, and a high price: about $2.80 per 25 g of protein at the $44.99 list price.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is Nuzest Protein Powder Good? The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, for a specific buyer. Nuzest Clean Lean Protein is a single-source pea protein isolate with a three-ingredient label in its vanilla flavor, no stevia, no gums, no added sugar and no dairy, gluten or soy, with routine third-party contaminant testing that the brand describes in more detail than most. For someone with a sensitive stomach, food allergies or a dislike of stevia aftertaste who wants a plant protein they can trust, it is one of the better choices on the market.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Nuzest Clean Lean Protein, Smooth Vanilla</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 25 g serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ingredients</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">About $2.81</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g at list price</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            It is not a good buy if price per gram of protein is what you care about, or if you need a sport certification. At the $44.99 list price for a 500 g tub of 20 servings, it costs about $2.81 per 25 g of protein, roughly double many pea proteins and four times Costco's whey. And because Nuzest's testing is the brand's own program, run through accredited labs, rather than a certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, it is not the choice for a drug-tested athlete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Label figures here are from Nuzest's US product page at the time of writing and vary by flavor; the brand says protein ranges from 18 g to 21 g per serving across flavors. Our view is based on the label, the brand's published testing claims and prices, not on tasting the product. Nothing here is medical advice; anyone with kidney disease, pregnancy, allergies or medication questions should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing among pea proteins more broadly, our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> rankings compare Nuzest with the cheaper and certified options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is in Nuzest Clean Lean Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Smooth Vanilla label lists three ingredients: pea protein isolate, natural flavors and thaumatin. A serving is two scoops, 25 g of powder, giving 20 g of protein and about 100 calories, per the label. That is 80 percent protein by weight, which is high for a flavored powder and reflects how little else is in the tub. A 500 g tub holds 20 servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thaumatin is the unusual part. It is a protein extracted from the fruit of the katemfe plant of West Africa and is intensely sweet, so very little is needed. Nuzest uses it instead of stevia, monk fruit or sucralose, which matters to the many buyers who find stevia bitter or who are trying to avoid it. Thaumatin is approved as a sweetener and flavor enhancer in the US and Europe. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> guide covers other options for stevia-averse buyers.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are no gums, thickeners, fillers or added sugars on the vanilla label. Nuzest describes the product as free of gluten, dairy and soy, made without GMOs, and lectin-free except the Chocolate Peanut Butter flavor. Other flavors add what you would expect: cocoa in the chocolate, coffee in Real Coffee, and so on. Just Natural, the unflavored version, is closest to plain pea protein isolate and suits cooking and baking.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pea itself, per Nuzest, is golden pea grown in Europe; the brand's articles say Northern France, in soils it describes as low in heavy metals. The brand lists seven flavors: Smooth Vanilla, Rich Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Wild Strawberry, Just Natural, Real Coffee and Salted Caramel.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the short label does not give you is texture help. Without gums or added fats, pea isolate tends to be thinner and slightly earthy in water, so many buyers blend it with milk, fruit or nut butter. That is a fair trade for a label this short, but it is worth knowing before you expect a creamy milkshake from water alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Nuzest Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, in the sense that Nuzest says its products are third-party tested, but not in the sense of a sport certification. Nuzest's product page says its products are made in GMP certified facilities and third-party tested by ISO 17025 accredited laboratories, with routine testing for microbial safety, gluten, dairy and soy, heavy metals, and pesticides and glyphosate. ISO 17025 is the international standard for testing-lab competence, so the claim is that accredited labs, not Nuzest's own staff, run the tests.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Nuzest does not have, as far as we could find, is a named certification from a third-party program: we found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project certification claimed on its US product page. The difference matters. A certification program sets the standard, chooses what to test, and publicly lists products that pass; a brand's own testing program, even through accredited labs, is the brand deciding what to test and how to report it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most buyers, the brand's program is reassuring. For drug-tested athletes, it is not enough, because banned-substance screening is not on Nuzest's listed tests and there is no public database entry to show a tester. If you compete under WADA rules or in a US college or pro league, choose a product listed by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport; our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists plant options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nuzest's articles also say it holds products back from sale until they meet its specifications, and that its testing schedule covers lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury. Those are the right metals to test for. What we did not find on the product page was a current batch-by-batch results table; Nuzest's own comparison article cites a certificate of analysis from December 2020, which is useful context but not a current batch.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TESTING VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Real contaminant testing through accredited labs, but no sport certification.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Good enough for everyday buyers who want a brand that tests for heavy metals and pesticides. Not enough for drug-tested athletes, who need an NSF or Informed Sport listing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Nuzest Protein Have Heavy Metals?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Like every pea protein, it can contain trace heavy metals, and Nuzest says so. Peas take up lead, cadmium and arsenic from soil, so plant proteins as a group test higher than whey. Nuzest's own articles acknowledge that heavy metal levels vary from batch to batch, and the product carries a California Proposition 65 warning, which is required when a daily serving could exceed California's very low threshold for lead or other listed chemicals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nuzest's answer is sourcing and testing: European golden peas from soil it describes as low in heavy metals, plus scheduled testing for lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury before release. That is a reasonable approach, and more than most plant brands describe. It is still the brand's account, so treat it as a strong claim rather than independent proof.
          </p>

          <p>
            Independent data is patchy. The Clean Label Project, an advocacy group, includes Nuzest's Smooth Vanilla and Just Natural in its protein powder dataset, but we could not see their results in the material available to us, and the project's methods have been criticized by NSF and industry groups. A 2025 Consumer Reports investigation into lead in protein powders and shakes found the highest levels mostly in plant-based products, which is the category context rather than a finding about Nuzest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practically, if heavy metals are your concern: choose a vanilla or unflavored flavor over chocolate, because cacao adds its own metals; ask Nuzest for a recent certificate of analysis for your batch; and do not stack several plant proteins and greens powders a day. Pregnant buyers should check any protein powder with their doctor or midwife. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> and <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guides go into the evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is 20 g of Pea Protein Enough?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most uses, 20 g is a reasonable serving, but it is on the low side for muscle building, and pea protein is slightly less efficient than whey. Research on muscle protein synthesis suggests that about 0.3 to 0.4 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per meal, or 20 to 40 g for most adults, gives a near-maximal response, with leucine content as an important driver. Twenty grams of pea protein supplies roughly 1.6 to 1.7 g of leucine, against about 2.2 g in 20 g of whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea protein's other weakness is methionine, an essential amino acid it is low in. Across a mixed diet that hardly matters, because grains, eggs, fish and other foods supply plenty. For a vegan diet built around legumes, mixing pea with rice or seed proteins over the day covers it. Nuzest is pea only, so it does not do that blending for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simple fix, if you are using Nuzest after training, is a slightly bigger serving. Two and a half scoops gives about 25 g of protein and roughly 2 g of leucine, close to a 20 g whey serving. That raises the cost per shake, which is already high, so it is a trade-off between label preference and price.
          </p>

          <p>
            For general health, weight management or topping up protein at breakfast, 20 g is plenty, and the short label and gentle sweetener are the reasons to choose Nuzest. For a plant athlete chasing maximum muscle per dollar, a pea and rice blend or a higher-dose pea powder will do the job for less; our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide ranks them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does Nuzest Cost per 25 g of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At its US list price, Nuzest Clean Lean Protein costs about $2.81 per 25 g of protein. The 500 g tub holds 20 servings of 20 g, which is 400 g of protein; $44.99 divided by 400, times 25, is $2.81. That puts it at the expensive end of the plant protein market and far above mainstream whey.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Nuzest option</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein in pack</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">500 g tub, list price</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400 g (20 x 20 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$44.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.81</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">500 g tub, 30% off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $31.49</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.97</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">250 g tub (10 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 g (10 x 20 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$25.99 if that is its price</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.25</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Nuzest discounts often. When we checked in late November 2026, its US site was running a Black Friday sale, with Buy One Get One Free on some sizes and flavors and 30 percent off everything else. At 30 percent off, the 500 g tub works out to about $1.97 per 25 g, which is still above average for pea protein but much easier to justify. Its product page also showed a $25.99 price for an option without stating the size; if that is the 250 g tub, it is about $3.25 per 25 g, which makes small tubs and single-serve sachets the worst value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against the alternatives, the premium is clear. Many mainstream pea and plant blends cost noticeably less per gram at list price, and Costco's whey costs about $0.70, as our <a href="/how-much-is-costco-protein-powder/">Costco protein powder price</a> breakdown shows. You are paying for the short label, the thaumatin, the European pea sourcing and the testing program.
          </p>

          <p>
            To keep the cost down, buy the 500 g tub during a sale, ideally two at once, and avoid the sachet packs except for travel. If you only use protein a few times a week, the premium per shake is small in absolute terms, and a clean label you will actually drink is worth more than a cheaper tub you leave in the cupboard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Should Buy Nuzest, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Nuzest if you want a plant protein with the shortest possible label and you are sensitive to the usual additives. People who react to gums, sugar alcohols, stevia or dairy often find single-source pea isolate with thaumatin easier to live with. It also suits people who want a brand that talks openly about heavy metal testing and sources its peas with that in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Nuzest if you are a drug-tested athlete, because it has no sport certification. Skip it if price per gram is your main concern, because it costs about twice as much as many plant powders at list price. And skip it if you want a big protein dose per scoop: at 20 g per two-scoop serving, you need more powder for a full post-workout serving than with most powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with brands we have reviewed, Nuzest sits closest to <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani</a> and <a href="/sunwarrior-protein-powder-review/">Sunwarrior</a> in the clean plant segment. Truvani is organic and also emphasizes heavy metal testing; Sunwarrior blends pea with other plant proteins. <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">Owyn</a> and <a href="/vega-protein-powder-review/">Vega</a> are cheaper per gram and easier to find in stores, with longer labels. Nuzest's distinguishing features are the three-ingredient vanilla and thaumatin.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a whey drinker curious about plant protein, start with a single 250 g tub or a sachet pack of Just Natural or Smooth Vanilla rather than a big tub, and mix it with milk or fruit at first. Pea isolate tastes and feels different from whey, and that first tub will tell you whether the short label is worth the premium for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating brand testing as a sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nuzest says accredited labs test for heavy metals, pesticides and microbes, but it has no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing we could find. Drug-tested athletes need a product in one of those databases.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming pea protein is metal-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Peas take up lead and cadmium from soil, and Nuzest acknowledges levels vary by batch and carries a Prop 65 warning. Choose vanilla or unflavored over chocolate, and ask for a recent certificate of analysis if it matters to you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing scoops instead of servings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Nuzest serving is two scoops, 25 g of powder, for 20 g of protein. If you use one scoop, you are getting about 10 g. Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not per scoop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying sachets or small tubs for everyday use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single-serve packs and the 250 g tub cost the most per gram. For daily use, buy the 500 g tub on sale; keep sachets for travel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a creamy shake from water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With no gums or added fats, Nuzest is thinner and earthier in water than most powders. Blend it with milk, banana or nut butter before deciding you dislike it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nuzest third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Nuzest says its products are made in GMP certified facilities and tested by ISO 17025 accredited third-party labs for heavy metals, pesticides and glyphosate, microbes, gluten, dairy and soy. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice certification, so it is not a choice for drug-tested athletes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nuzest protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label, it is one of the cleanest: the Smooth Vanilla lists pea protein isolate, natural flavors and thaumatin only, with no added sugar, gums, stevia or dairy. It carries a Prop 65 warning, as many plant proteins do. It is a supplement, not a substitute for a varied diet.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Nuzest have heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Like all pea proteins it can contain traces, and Nuzest acknowledges levels vary by batch. It says it tests for lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury and sources European golden peas from low-metal soils. Choose vanilla or unflavored over chocolate, and ask the brand for a recent certificate of analysis.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Nuzest use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Thaumatin, a naturally sweet protein extracted from the katemfe fruit. Nuzest uses it instead of stevia, monk fruit or sucralose. That makes it a rare option for buyers who dislike stevia's aftertaste. Just Natural, the unflavored version, is unsweetened.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Nuzest Clean Lean Protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  20 g per two-scoop, 25 g serving in Smooth Vanilla, with about 100 calories, per the label at the time of writing. Nuzest says protein ranges from 18 g to 21 g per serving depending on flavor. A 500 g tub holds 20 servings.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Nuzest flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Nuzest does not rank its flavors and we have not tasted them. Smooth Vanilla and Just Natural are the most versatile and have the shortest labels; Just Natural is the one to choose for baking and cooking. Chocolate flavors are likely to carry more trace metals because of the cacao.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nuzest worth the price?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At list price, only if the short label and testing matter to you: it costs about $2.81 per 25 g of protein, roughly double many plant powders. On a 30 percent sale it is about $1.97, which is much easier to justify for a sensitive stomach or a stevia-free diet.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

